claim:talking_heads--parliament_funkadelic--attributed a riff:InfluenceClaim ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talking_Heads> ;
    riff:describes influence:talking_heads--parliament_funkadelic ;
    riff:evidenceQuote "They collaborated with the British producer Brian Eno on the acclaimed albums More Songs About Buildings and Food (1978), Fear of Music (1979), and Remain in Light (1980), which blended their art school sensibilities with influence from artists such as Parliament-Funkadelic and Fela Kuti."@en ;
    riff:hasEvidenceKind riff:Attributed .

claim:talking_heads--roxy_music--attributed a riff:InfluenceClaim ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roxy_Music> ;
    riff:describes influence:talking_heads--roxy_music ;
    riff:evidenceQuote "They were an influence on the early 1980s British new romantic-movement with bands such as Ultravox, Visage and Spandau Ballet, as well as new wave art rock-bands such as Magazine, Cabaret Voltaire, and the American bands Devo and Talking Heads."@en ;
    riff:hasEvidenceKind riff:Attributed .

claim:tangerine_dream--jimi_hendrix--attributed a riff:InfluenceClaim ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine_Dream> ;
    riff:describes influence:tangerine_dream--jimi_hendrix ;
    riff:evidenceQuote "Tangerine Dream began as a surreal krautrock band, with each of the members contributing different musical influences and styles, before becoming a \"revered progressive electronic act.\" Edgar Froese's guitar style was inspired by Jimi Hendrix, as well as the avant-garde composers Iannis Xenakis and Karlheinz Stockhausen, while Christopher Franke contributed elements of György Ligeti and Terry Riley."@en ;
    riff:hasEvidenceKind riff:Attributed .

claim:tangerine_dream--pink_floyd--attributed a riff:InfluenceClaim ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine_Dream> ;
    riff:describes influence:tangerine_dream--pink_floyd ;
    riff:evidenceQuote "Pink Floyd were also an influence on Edgar Froese and Tangerine Dream, the band in its very early psychedelic rock band phase playing improvisations based on Pink Floyd's \"Interstellar Overdrive\"."@en ;
    riff:hasEvidenceKind riff:Attributed .

claim:tangerine_dream--yes--attributed a riff:InfluenceClaim ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine_Dream> ;
    riff:describes influence:tangerine_dream--yes ;
    riff:evidenceQuote "Yes-like progressive rock influence was brought in by Steve Jolliffe on Cyclone."@en ;
    riff:hasEvidenceKind riff:Attributed .

claim:the_beatles--bo_diddley--attributed a riff:InfluenceClaim ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bo_Diddley> ;
    riff:describes influence:the_beatles--bo_diddley ;
    riff:evidenceQuote "He influenced many artists, including Buddy Holly, the Beatles, the Rolling Stones, the Animals, George Thorogood, Syd Barrett, Tom Petty, and the Clash."@en ;
    riff:hasEvidenceKind riff:Attributed .

claim:the_beatles--bob_dylan--attributed a riff:InfluenceClaim ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Beatles> ;
    riff:describes influence:the_beatles--bob_dylan ;
    riff:evidenceQuote "The Beatles continued to absorb influences long after their initial success, finding new musical and lyrical avenues from thir contemporaries, including Bob Dylan, Smokey Robinson and the Miracles, the Who, Frank Zappa, the Lovin' Spoonful, the Byrds and the Beach Boys, whose 1966 album Pet Sounds amazed and inspired McCartney."@en ;
    riff:hasEvidenceKind riff:Attributed .

